  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-46534",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor $5$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n45{m}^{2}+5=5(9{m}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(9{m}^{2}+1).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{m}^{2}+1 & =9{m}^{2}-(-1) \\\\  & =(3m)^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =(3m+\\text{i})(3m-\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n5(9{m}^{2}+1)=5(3m+\\text{i})(3m-\\text{i}).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
